 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes are delivered over self-hosted WebSockets: the server pushes revisions to connected browsers over connections it owns and operates itself, rather than polling for them or routing them through a hosted realtime vendor.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed around this choice. The change store must keep revisions replayable so a reconnecting session can catch up, and publish each committed revision reliably to delivery. Delivery owns socket session lifecycle — accepting connections, authorizing them, and pushing revisions as they commit. Browsers carry reconnect and catch-up logic: on disconnect they must detect the gap, reconnect, and request replay up to the latest revision rather than assuming an unchanged board.
 
 ## Architecture
 
 - Access: `docs/intent/access/access-design.md`, with `docs/intent/access/access-specs.md`. Written below the stable HTTPS command boundary.
-- Change store: `docs/intent/change-store/change-store-design.md`, with `docs/intent/change-store/change-store-specs.md`. Not written yet. Revision history, read indexes, and any publication outbox depend on the delivery approach.
-- Delivery: `docs/intent/delivery/delivery-design.md`, with `docs/intent/delivery/delivery-specs.md`. Not written yet. Owns polling responses, socket sessions, or hosted publication, depending on the approach.
-- Browser sync: `docs/intent/browser-sync/browser-sync-design.md`, with `docs/intent/browser-sync/browser-sync-specs.md`. Not written yet. Its subscription, reconnect, and catch-up behavior depend on the approach.
+- Change store: `docs/intent/change-store/change-store-design.md`, with `docs/intent/change-store/change-store-specs.md`. Not written yet. Owns replayable revision history and reliable publication of each committed revision to delivery.
+- Delivery: `docs/intent/delivery/delivery-design.md`, with `docs/intent/delivery/delivery-specs.md`. Not written yet. Owns self-hosted WebSocket session lifecycle: accepting, authorizing, and maintaining connections, and pushing committed revisions to them.
+- Browser sync: `docs/intent/browser-sync/browser-sync-design.md`, with `docs/intent/browser-sync/browser-sync-specs.md`. Not written yet. Owns the browser's subscription to its socket session, and reconnect/catch-up logic after a disconnect.
 
